{
  "note": "Record regions, not countries. A region is a piece of ground whose records were filed the same way, and an era is a stretch of years over which that stayed true. The regime bands are the researched sovereignty of the Defranceski archive; what is new here is `filedUnder` — which archive a regime actually MEANS. That is the whole point: knowing Gračišće was Italian in 1930 is trivia until it tells you to open FamilySearch's Pola and Trieste collection rather than its Croatian one. The regions are chosen by where research is actually being done rather than by what would look impressive on a map. Each one was written because somebody was stuck on that ground and needed to know whose archive to write to.",
  "precision": "The rings are approximate — deliberately. They exist to shade which filing system a place fell under, not to adjudicate a border. A place near a ring's edge should be checked against both neighbours.",
